{"authored" [{"question" "q-71446" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The intersection of the planes $x+2y+3z=0$ and $3x+2y+z+8=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{a}_{1},0,{a}_{3}⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{1}+{a}_{3},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f4af09bdba027e1c8d2692408d6a5dca614ddb224d8bc03df777b05e50c705" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-71446" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $y=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation gives    \n\n$$\nx+2(0)+3z=0\\,⟹\\,x=-3z,\n$$\n\nand substituting $y=0$ and $x=-3z$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+2y+z+8 & =0 \\\\ 3(-3z)+2(0)+z+8 & =0 \\\\ -8z & =-8 \\\\ z & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-3z=-3$ and $a=⟨-3,0,1⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $x+2y+3z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,2,3⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $3x+2y+z+8=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨3,2,1⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,2,3⟩\\times ⟨3,2,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-4i+8j-4k \\\\  & =⟨-4,8,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can pick any vector that is parallel to ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}$ as a direction vector of the line. So, let \n\n$$\nb=-\\frac{1}{4}({n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2})=⟨1,-2,1⟩.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨-3,0,1⟩+t⟨1,-2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=-3+1=-2,$ and $b=⟨1,-2,1⟩.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f4af09bdba027e1c8d2692408d6a5dca614ddb224d8bc03df777b05e50c705" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-71446" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b4755c946daaa3f3b9f6bf1768006166fe4c9b3c3f5b01006e968cbbf83917a7" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-71446" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f4af09bdba027e1c8d2692408d6a5dca614ddb224d8bc03df777b05e50c705" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-71446" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=-2,\\,b=⟨1,-2,1⟩"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f4af09bdba027e1c8d2692408d6a5dca614ddb224d8bc03df777b05e50c705" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-71446" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=-3,\\,b=⟨1,-2,3⟩"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f4af09bdba027e1c8d2692408d6a5dca614ddb224d8bc03df777b05e50c705" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-71446" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=2,\\,b=⟨1,2,1⟩"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f4af09bdba027e1c8d2692408d6a5dca614ddb224d8bc03df777b05e50c705" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-71446" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=2,\\,b=⟨2,-2,3⟩"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f4af09bdba027e1c8d2692408d6a5dca614ddb224d8bc03df777b05e50c705" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-71446" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=3,\\,b=⟨-1,-2,-3⟩"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f4af09bdba027e1c8d2692408d6a5dca614ddb224d8bc03df777b05e50c705" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-71446" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=-2,\\,b=⟨1,-2,1⟩"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f4af09bdba027e1c8d2692408d6a5dca614ddb224d8bc03df777b05e50c705" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129238" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The intersection of the planes $x+y-z-3=0$ and $x+2y-3z+18=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{a}_{1},{a}_{2},0⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f4af09bdba027e1c8d2692408d6a5dca614ddb224d8bc03df777b05e50c705" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129238" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $z=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation gives  \n\n$$\nx+y-0-3=0\\,⟹\\,x=3-y,\n$$\n\nand substituting $z=0$ and $x=3-y$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3-y)+2y-3(0)+18 & =0 \\\\ 3+y+18 & =0 \\\\ y & =-21.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=3-y=3+21=24$ and $a=⟨24,-21,0⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $x+y-z-3=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,1,-1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x+2y-3z+18=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,2,-3⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,1,-1⟩\\times ⟨1,2,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i+2j+k \\\\  & =⟨-1,2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨24,-21,0⟩+t⟨-1,2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=24-21=3,$ and $b=⟨-1,2,1⟩.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f4af09bdba027e1c8d2692408d6a5dca614ddb224d8bc03df777b05e50c705" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129238" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b4755c946daaa3f3b9f6bf1768006166fe4c9b3c3f5b01006e968cbbf83917a7" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129238" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f4af09bdba027e1c8d2692408d6a5dca614ddb224d8bc03df777b05e50c705" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129238" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-3,\\,b=⟨-1,1,2⟩"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f4af09bdba027e1c8d2692408d6a5dca614ddb224d8bc03df777b05e50c705" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129238" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-6,\\,b=⟨-2,1,-1⟩"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f4af09bdba027e1c8d2692408d6a5dca614ddb224d8bc03df777b05e50c705" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129238" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=3,\\,b=⟨-1,2,1⟩"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f4af09bdba027e1c8d2692408d6a5dca614ddb224d8bc03df777b05e50c705" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129238" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=5,\\,b=⟨1,2,1⟩"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f4af09bdba027e1c8d2692408d6a5dca614ddb224d8bc03df777b05e50c705" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129238" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=6,\\,b=⟨-2,1,1⟩"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f4af09bdba027e1c8d2692408d6a5dca614ddb224d8bc03df777b05e50c705" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-71463" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the two planes $3x+3y+2z=0$ and $x-y-z=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $⟨1,6,-5⟩.$\n- The planes are parallel.\n- The planes intersect on a line." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f4af09bdba027e1c8d2692408d6a5dca614ddb224d8bc03df777b05e50c705" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-71463" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $3x+3y+2z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨3,3,2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x-y-z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,-1,-1⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨3,3,2⟩\\times ⟨1,-1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i+5j-6k \\\\  & =⟨-1,5,-6⟩\\ne ⟨1,6,-5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. As a result, the corresponding planes are not parallel either.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, two non-parallel planes in $3$-dimensional space must intersect on a line.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f4af09bdba027e1c8d2692408d6a5dca614ddb224d8bc03df777b05e50c705" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-71463" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b4755c946daaa3f3b9f6bf1768006166fe4c9b3c3f5b01006e968cbbf83917a7" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-71463" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f4af09bdba027e1c8d2692408d6a5dca614ddb224d8bc03df777b05e50c705" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-71463" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f4af09bdba027e1c8d2692408d6a5dca614ddb224d8bc03df777b05e50c705" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-71463" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f4af09bdba027e1c8d2692408d6a5dca614ddb224d8bc03df777b05e50c705" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-71463" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f4af09bdba027e1c8d2692408d6a5dca614ddb224d8bc03df777b05e50c705" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-71463" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f4af09bdba027e1c8d2692408d6a5dca614ddb224d8bc03df777b05e50c705" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-71463" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f4af09bdba027e1c8d2692408d6a5dca614ddb224d8bc03df777b05e50c705" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-71463" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7f4af09bdba027e1c8d2692408d6a5dca614ddb224d8bc03df777b05e50c705" "basis" 1580 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042850/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-71446" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The intersection of the planes $x+2y+3z=0$ and $3x+2y+z+8=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{a}_{1},0,{a}_{3}⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{1}+{a}_{3},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064732/state.json" "file_sha256" "df1b25de21888bab782d48f1489cba6e177e043a420e42ac637a8984d928cd01"} {"question" "q-71446" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $y=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation gives    \n\n$$\nx+2(0)+3z=0\\,⟹\\,x=-3z,\n$$\n\nand substituting $y=0$ and $x=-3z$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+2y+z+8 & =0 \\\\ 3(-3z)+2(0)+z+8 & =0 \\\\ -8z & =-8 \\\\ z & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-3z=-3$ and $a=⟨-3,0,1⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $x+2y+3z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,2,3⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $3x+2y+z+8=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨3,2,1⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,2,3⟩\\times ⟨3,2,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-4i+8j-4k \\\\  & =⟨-4,8,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can pick any vector that is parallel to ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}$ as a direction vector of the line. So, let \n\n$$\nb=-\\frac{1}{4}({n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2})=⟨1,-2,1⟩.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨-3,0,1⟩+t⟨1,-2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=-3+1=-2,$ and $b=⟨1,-2,1⟩.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064732/state.json" "file_sha256" "df1b25de21888bab782d48f1489cba6e177e043a420e42ac637a8984d928cd01"} {"question" "q-71446" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064732/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6652e29795ce25bf19f9a8f2969f5f9d99535dc692c635f88d0b7fb3e53ed6d0"} {"question" "q-71446" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064732/state.json" "file_sha256" "df1b25de21888bab782d48f1489cba6e177e043a420e42ac637a8984d928cd01"} {"question" "q-71446" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=-2,\\,b=⟨1,-2,1⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=-3,\\,b=⟨1,-2,3⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=2,\\,b=⟨1,2,1⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=2,\\,b=⟨2,-2,3⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=3,\\,b=⟨-1,-2,-3⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064732/state.json" "file_sha256" "df1b25de21888bab782d48f1489cba6e177e043a420e42ac637a8984d928cd01"} {"question" "q-71446" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=-2,\\,b=⟨1,-2,1⟩"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064732/state.json" "file_sha256" "df1b25de21888bab782d48f1489cba6e177e043a420e42ac637a8984d928cd01"} {"question" "q-71463" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the two planes $3x+3y+2z=0$ and $x-y-z=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $⟨1,6,-5⟩.$\n- The planes are parallel.\n- The planes intersect on a line." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064732/state.json" "file_sha256" "df1b25de21888bab782d48f1489cba6e177e043a420e42ac637a8984d928cd01"} {"question" "q-71463" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $3x+3y+2z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨3,3,2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x-y-z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,-1,-1⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨3,3,2⟩\\times ⟨1,-1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i+5j-6k \\\\  & =⟨-1,5,-6⟩\\ne ⟨1,6,-5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. As a result, the corresponding planes are not parallel either.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, two non-parallel planes in $3$-dimensional space must intersect on a line.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064732/state.json" "file_sha256" "df1b25de21888bab782d48f1489cba6e177e043a420e42ac637a8984d928cd01"} {"question" "q-71463" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064732/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6652e29795ce25bf19f9a8f2969f5f9d99535dc692c635f88d0b7fb3e53ed6d0"} {"question" "q-71463" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064732/state.json" "file_sha256" "df1b25de21888bab782d48f1489cba6e177e043a420e42ac637a8984d928cd01"} {"question" "q-71463" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064732/state.json" "file_sha256" "df1b25de21888bab782d48f1489cba6e177e043a420e42ac637a8984d928cd01"} {"question" "q-71463" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064732/state.json" "file_sha256" "df1b25de21888bab782d48f1489cba6e177e043a420e42ac637a8984d928cd01"} {"question" "q-129238" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The intersection of the planes $x+y-z-3=0$ and $x+2y-3z+18=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{a}_{1},{a}_{2},0⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064732/state.json" "file_sha256" "df1b25de21888bab782d48f1489cba6e177e043a420e42ac637a8984d928cd01"} {"question" "q-129238" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $z=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation gives  \n\n$$\nx+y-0-3=0\\,⟹\\,x=3-y,\n$$\n\nand substituting $z=0$ and $x=3-y$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3-y)+2y-3(0)+18 & =0 \\\\ 3+y+18 & =0 \\\\ y & =-21.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=3-y=3+21=24$ and $a=⟨24,-21,0⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $x+y-z-3=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,1,-1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x+2y-3z+18=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,2,-3⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,1,-1⟩\\times ⟨1,2,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i+2j+k \\\\  & =⟨-1,2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨24,-21,0⟩+t⟨-1,2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=24-21=3,$ and $b=⟨-1,2,1⟩.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064732/state.json" "file_sha256" "df1b25de21888bab782d48f1489cba6e177e043a420e42ac637a8984d928cd01"} {"question" "q-129238" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064732/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6652e29795ce25bf19f9a8f2969f5f9d99535dc692c635f88d0b7fb3e53ed6d0"} {"question" "q-129238" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064732/state.json" "file_sha256" "df1b25de21888bab782d48f1489cba6e177e043a420e42ac637a8984d928cd01"} {"question" "q-129238" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-3,\\,b=⟨-1,1,2⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-6,\\,b=⟨-2,1,-1⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=3,\\,b=⟨-1,2,1⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=5,\\,b=⟨1,2,1⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=6,\\,b=⟨-2,1,1⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064732/state.json" "file_sha256" "df1b25de21888bab782d48f1489cba6e177e043a420e42ac637a8984d928cd01"} {"question" "q-129238" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=3,\\,b=⟨-1,2,1⟩"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064732/state.json" "file_sha256" "df1b25de21888bab782d48f1489cba6e177e043a420e42ac637a8984d928cd01"} {"question" "q-129258" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the two planes $x-5y+2z=0$ and $3x-2y+7=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The normal vectors of the planes are parallel.\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $0.$\n- The planes intersect on a line." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064732/state.json" "file_sha256" "df1b25de21888bab782d48f1489cba6e177e043a420e42ac637a8984d928cd01"} {"question" "q-129258" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x-5y+2z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,-5,2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $3x-2y+7=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨3,-2,0⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,-5,2⟩\\times ⟨3,-2,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =4i+6j+13k \\\\  & =⟨4,6,13⟩\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, two non-parallel planes in $3$-dimensional space must intersect on a line.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064732/state.json" "file_sha256" "df1b25de21888bab782d48f1489cba6e177e043a420e42ac637a8984d928cd01"} {"question" "q-129258" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064732/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6652e29795ce25bf19f9a8f2969f5f9d99535dc692c635f88d0b7fb3e53ed6d0"} {"question" "q-129258" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064732/state.json" "file_sha256" "df1b25de21888bab782d48f1489cba6e177e043a420e42ac637a8984d928cd01"} {"question" "q-129258" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064732/state.json" "file_sha256" "df1b25de21888bab782d48f1489cba6e177e043a420e42ac637a8984d928cd01"} {"question" "q-129258" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064732/state.json" "file_sha256" "df1b25de21888bab782d48f1489cba6e177e043a420e42ac637a8984d928cd01"} {"question" "q-129260" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the two planes $3x+y-2z=0$ and $2x+y+3z+7=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $⟨5,-13,1⟩.$\n- The planes are parallel.\n- The planes intersect on a line." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064732/state.json" "file_sha256" "df1b25de21888bab782d48f1489cba6e177e043a420e42ac637a8984d928cd01"} {"question" "q-129260" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $3x+y-2z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨3,1,-2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $2x+y+3z+7=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨2,1,3⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨3,1,-2⟩\\times ⟨2,1,3⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =5i-13j+k \\\\  & =⟨5,-13,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. As a result, the corresponding planes are not parallel either.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, two non-parallel planes in $3$-dimensional space must intersect on a line.\n\nTherefore, only I and III only are true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064732/state.json" "file_sha256" "df1b25de21888bab782d48f1489cba6e177e043a420e42ac637a8984d928cd01"} {"question" "q-129260" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064732/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6652e29795ce25bf19f9a8f2969f5f9d99535dc692c635f88d0b7fb3e53ed6d0"} {"question" "q-129260" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064732/state.json" "file_sha256" "df1b25de21888bab782d48f1489cba6e177e043a420e42ac637a8984d928cd01"} {"question" "q-129260" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064732/state.json" "file_sha256" "df1b25de21888bab782d48f1489cba6e177e043a420e42ac637a8984d928cd01"} {"question" "q-129260" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064732/state.json" "file_sha256" "df1b25de21888bab782d48f1489cba6e177e043a420e42ac637a8984d928cd01"}] "usage" {"q-129238" [] "q-71446" [] "q-71463" []} "basis" 2118 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [17592186317927 17592186317928 17592186317929 17592186317930 17592186317931 17592186317948 17592186317949 17592186317950 17592186317951 17592186317952 17592186317955 17592186317956 17592186317957 17592186317958 17592186317959] "immutable_fields" [[17592186317926 :answer-field/correct] [17592186317926 :answer-field/id] [17592186317926 :answer-field/key] [17592186317926 :answer-field/type] [17592186317947 :answer-field/correct] [17592186317947 :answer-field/id] [17592186317947 :answer-field/key] [17592186317947 :answer-field/type] [17592186317954 :answer-field/correct] [17592186317954 :answer-field/id] [17592186317954 :answer-field/key] [17592186317954 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
